<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poitiers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lemonum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Poitiers is a city of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the Clain river, the historic capital of Poitou. As Lemonum it was the chief town of the Gallic Pictones, and it became a Roman city and early bishopric associated with Saint Hilary. Near it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> defeated an Umayyad army in 732, and the counts of Poitou and dukes of Aquitaine ruled from it. Its Romanesque churches include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-la-grande">Notre-Dame-la-Grande</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poitiers.jpg" alt="Poitiers" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vermario at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poitiers_hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Martel's victory near Tours and <b>Poitiers</b> in 732 CE checked an Umayyad raid into Gaul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Poitevin house, often called the Ramnulfids, prevailed in the mid-tenth century under William III Towhead and held the duchy for nearly two centuries, ruling from <b>Poitiers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The mayors of the palace restored it by force: Charles Martel defeated an Umayyad army under Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi between <b>Poitiers</b> and Tours, usually dated 732, and his son Pippin the Short subdued Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Raids beyond the Pyrenees were checked by Charles Martel at the Battle of Tours (<b>Poitiers</b>) in 732.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Franks under Clovis I ended this expansion: in 507, allied with the Burgundians, they killed Alaric II at the Battle of Campus Vogladensis near <b>Poitiers</b> and sacked Toulouse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the same year the prince captured King John II of France at <b>Poitiers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-4">4 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
